const AX_2 = {

/* ============================== ELECTRODYNAMICS ============================== */
em: {
 axioms:[
  {n:"Gauss's law for the electric field", s:R`\nabla\cdot\mathbf{E}=\frac{\rho}{\varepsilon_{0}}`,
   t:'Charge is the sole source of the longitudinal field, with a 1/r² falloff exact to the precision of Coulomb-law null tests (Williams, Faller & Hill 1971: any deviation exponent is below 3 × 10⁻¹⁶, equivalently a photon mass under 10⁻¹⁴ eV). Independent of the other three: nothing in Faraday or Ampere fixes the coupling of charge to field.'},
  {n:'Absence of magnetic charge', s:R`\nabla\cdot\mathbf{B}=0`,
   t:"An empirical postulate, not a necessity. Maxwell's equations extend consistently to include magnetic sources, and Dirac showed in 1931 that a single monopole would force electric charge quantisation, \\(eg=2\\pi n\\hbar\\). Dropping it costs nothing formally and would explain something the theory otherwise leaves brute; no monopole has been found."},
  {n:"Faraday's law of induction", s:R`\nabla\times\mathbf{E}=-\frac{\partial\mathbf{B}}{\partial t}`,
   t:'A changing magnetic field circulates E. With the second postulate it is the statement that the field strength is the exterior derivative of a potential, \\(F=dA\\), so these two are the kinematic half of the theory and carry no coupling constant. The "flux rule" taught in textbooks is not this law but a consequence of it plus the Lorentz force.'},
  {n:'Ampere–Maxwell law', s:R`\nabla\times\mathbf{B}=\mu_{0}\mathbf{J}+\mu_{0}\varepsilon_{0}\frac{\partial\mathbf{E}}{\partial t}`,
   t:"Maxwell's displacement current. Taking the divergence gives \\(\\partial_t\\rho+\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{J}=0\\): charge conservation is a **theorem** of the field equations, not a separate axiom, and the term was added precisely to make the system consistent with it. It is also what makes vacuum wave solutions exist at all."},
  {n:'Lorentz force law', s:R`\mathbf{F}=q\left(\mathbf{E}+\mathbf{v}\times\mathbf{B}\right)`,
   t:'Logically independent of the four field equations, which say only how charges make fields and are silent on how fields move charges. No derivation of it from them exists; attempts to derive it from energy-momentum conservation smuggle in the field stress tensor, which already presupposes it. Without this postulate the field equations have no observational content whatever.'},
  {n:'Constitutive relations', s:R`\mathbf{D}=\varepsilon\mathbf{E},\qquad \mathbf{B}=\mu\mathbf{H},\qquad \mathbf{J}=\sigma\mathbf{E}`,
   t:'Where matter enters, and the only place it does. The macroscopic equations are vacuum Maxwell plus a material model; ε, μ, σ are neither predicted nor constrained by the theory and must be measured or supplied by a microscopic theory. Everything from optics to circuit design lives in the choice made here.'},
  {n:'Dynamics for the sources', s:R`m\frac{d}{dt}\left(\gamma\mathbf{v}\right)=\mathbf{F}_{\rm Lorentz}+\mathbf{F}_{\rm rad}`,
   t:'The system does not close without an equation of motion for the charges. Adding the radiation-reaction term \\(\\mathbf{F}_{\\rm rad}=\\mu_0q^2\\dot{\\mathbf{a}}/6\\pi c\\) to enforce energy balance produces third-order equations with runaway and pre-accelerating solutions. Classical electrodynamics has no consistent closed dynamics for point charges; this is a defect of the axiom set, not of the solutions.'}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'The electromagnetic field is a dynamical entity, not a summary of forces between charges', k:'core',
   b:'The decisive argument is not metaphysical but dynamical: for two charges in relative motion the instantaneous forces are not equal and opposite, so mechanical momentum alone is not conserved. Assigning momentum density \\(\\varepsilon_0\\mathbf{E}\\times\\mathbf{B}\\) restores conservation exactly. The field carries energy, momentum and angular momentum, and the theory is false if it does not.'},
  {h:'The luminiferous ether: a material medium whose elastic deformations are the fields', k:'abandoned',
   b:"Load-bearing for fifty years. Maxwell derived his equations from a mechanical model of vortices and idle wheels; Kelvin, Stokes and Lorentz all built ether theories, and Fresnel's drag coefficient \\(1-1/n^2\\) was confirmed by Fizeau in 1851. Michelson–Morley (1887) found no fringe shift at the 1/40-fringe level against an expected 0.4. Replaced by the field on Minkowski spacetime: the equations survived, the substrate did not."},
  {h:'Constitutive relations are linear, local, instantaneous and isotropic', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Independently revisable, and revised everywhere: dispersion makes ε frequency-dependent (Kramers–Kronig ties its real and imaginary parts), crystals make it a tensor, intense fields make it nonlinear (second-harmonic generation, Franken 1961), plasmas and metamaterials make it spatially nonlocal. Each repair is local to the material model and leaves the field equations untouched — the standard signature of a Duhemian auxiliary.'},
  {h:'Charges are point-like, or have rigid finite structure', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Needed to define \\(\\rho\\) and to write the Lorentz force for a particle. Point charges give an infinite self-energy; rigid finite charges are incompatible with relativity (no rigid bodies) and produce the Abraham–Lorentz 4/3 factor in the electromagnetic mass. Poincare stresses were invented to patch this. Every classical resolution buys consistency by adding structure the theory cannot itself justify.'},
  {h:'Fields are measured by the force on a vanishingly small, vanishingly weak test charge', k:'bridge',
   b:"The correspondence rule that gives E and B empirical content. It is idealised in two directions at once: the charge must be small enough not to disturb the source distribution and small enough that its own radiation reaction is negligible, but large enough to measure. In practice ε₀ and μ₀ were fixed by capacitance and by the force between current-carrying wires — Weber and Kohlrausch's 1856 ratio of units, 3.107 × 10⁸ m/s, is the measurement that made the wave prediction possible."},
  {h:'No magnetic monopoles exist anywhere', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Stated as \\(\\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf{B}=0\\) it looks axiomatic, but it is an empirical claim revisable at no structural cost. Grand unified theories predict monopoles of mass 10¹⁶–10¹⁷ GeV; inflation was in part motivated by their non-observation. MACRO set a cosmic flux bound near 10⁻¹⁶ cm⁻² s⁻¹ sr⁻¹, and MoEDAL at the LHC excludes production of monopoles up to a few TeV.'},
  {h:'Gauge freedom is surplus structure; only E and B are physical', k:'heuristic',
   b:'Guided the whole of twentieth-century field theory — the gauge principle generalised to Yang–Mills and to the Standard Model. It is not itself under test in classical electrodynamics, where A is unobservable by construction. The Aharonov–Bohm effect (1959, Chambers 1960) complicates it: the holonomy \\(\\oint\\mathbf{A}\\cdot d\\mathbf{l}\\) is gauge-invariant, physical, and non-local, so "surplus" is the wrong word even if "real" is too strong.'}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:'Transverse electromagnetic waves exist in vacuum and travel at a speed fixed by two laboratory constants of electrostatics and magnetostatics', num:R`c=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\mu_{0}\varepsilon_{0}}}=2.998\times10^{8}\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'The paradigm novel prediction. Weber and Kohlrausch measured the ratio of units in 1856 as 3.107 × 10⁸ m/s; Fizeau had light at 3.15 × 10⁸ m/s in 1849; Maxwell drew the inference in 1862. Hertz generated and detected metre-wavelength waves in 1887–88, showing reflection, refraction, polarisation and standing waves, twenty-three years after the theory. A propagation speed differing measurably from the optical one would have refuted it.'},
  {p:'Light exerts pressure on an absorbing surface equal to the incident intensity divided by c, and twice that on a perfect mirror', num:R`P=\frac{I}{c}\ \ \Rightarrow\ \ 4.5\ \mu\mathrm{Pa}\ \text{for sunlight at 1 AU}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Maxwell predicted it in the Treatise (1873) from the field momentum density. Lebedev measured it on torsion-suspended vanes in 1901, agreeing within roughly twenty per cent; Nichols and Hull, working through 1901–03 with better control of radiometric convection, got agreement at the few-per-cent level. Null radiation pressure would have destroyed the case for the field as a momentum carrier and with it the argument in the first hypothesis above.'},
  {p:"The Fresnel reflection and refraction coefficients and Brewster's angle follow from the boundary conditions on E and B at an interface", num:R`r_{\perp}=\frac{n_{1}\cos\theta_{i}-n_{2}\cos\theta_{t}}{n_{1}\cos\theta_{i}+n_{2}\cos\theta_{t}},\qquad \tan\theta_{B}=n_{2}/n_{1}`, st:'retrodiction',
   b:"**This is a retrodiction and should not be counted otherwise.** Fresnel derived these formulae in 1823 from an elastic-solid ether, and Brewster had his angle empirically in 1815. Maxwell's theory recovers them from continuity of the tangential fields, with no new parameters. Worrall built structural realism on exactly this transition: the equations were retained while the ontology that produced them was discarded."},
  {p:'The refractive index of a transparent medium equals the square root of its static dielectric constant', num:R`n=\sqrt{\varepsilon_{r}\mu_{r}}\approx\sqrt{\varepsilon_{r}}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Maxwell checked it against paraffin wax: √ε = 1.405 against a measured index of 1.422, which he found encouraging. It fails badly elsewhere — water has ε_r ≈ 80, so the prediction is n = 8.9 against a measured 1.33. The repair was to abandon the static value: ε is frequency-dependent, and the Lorentz oscillator model of dispersion (1878–1900) reconciles the two by relating the optical and static regimes through resonances.'},
  {p:'An accelerated charge radiates, so an electron in a Rutherford atom must spiral into the nucleus', num:R`P=\frac{\mu_{0}q^{2}a^{2}}{6\pi c},\qquad \tau_{\rm collapse}\approx1.6\times10^{-11}\ \mathrm{s}`, st:'failed',
   b:"An unambiguous consequence of the axioms, and flatly false: matter exists. Larmor's formula (1897) applied to the 1911 nuclear atom gives collapse from the Bohr radius in about sixteen picoseconds, with a continuously reddening spectrum rather than sharp lines. Nothing was done about it within classical electrodynamics; Bohr's 1913 quantisation postulate simply forbade the radiation, and the failure is one of the three that forced quantum theory."},
  {p:"The energy of a point charge's own field, and the motion that field produces on the charge", num:R`U_{\rm self}=\frac{e^{2}}{8\pi\varepsilon_{0}a}\to\infty,\qquad \tau_{\rm AL}=\frac{2r_{e}}{3c}=6.3\times10^{-24}\ \mathrm{s}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Setting the self-energy equal to \\(m_ec^2\\) gives the classical electron radius 2.818 × 10⁻¹⁵ m, but electron scattering shows no structure down to 10⁻¹⁸ m, so the cutoff is not physical. The Abraham–Lorentz equation admits self-accelerating runaways and, when those are excluded, pre-acceleration on the timescale τ_AL. The Landau–Lifshitz reduction of order tames it perturbatively; no consistent classical theory of point charges exists. QED renormalises the divergence rather than removing it.'},
  {p:'No magnetic monopole will ever be detected', num:R`\Phi_{\rm monopole}<1.4\times10^{-16}\ \mathrm{cm^{-2}s^{-1}sr^{-1}}`, st:'open',
   b:"A standing negative prediction with active tests. MACRO at Gran Sasso reported the flux bound above for GUT-scale monopoles over 1989–2000; IceCube extends limits to relativistic monopoles; MoEDAL at the LHC searches for pair production directly. A single confirmed detection would require adding magnetic sources to two of the four field equations and would, via Dirac's argument, explain the quantisation of electric charge — which classical electrodynamics otherwise leaves unexplained."}
 ]
},

/* ============================== CLASSICAL MECHANICS ============================== */
cm: {
 axioms:[
  {n:'Absolute space and absolute time', s:R`\text{Space is a fixed Euclidean 3-manifold and time flows uniformly, independently of anything in them.}`,
   t:"Newton's Scholium states it explicitly, and the rotating-bucket argument is offered in its defence. Leibniz, Berkeley and Mach rejected it; Neumann and Lange showed only an inertial structure is needed. Galilean spacetime — absolute simultaneity and absolute acceleration, no absolute position or velocity — is the modern replacement, and special relativity then removes absolute simultaneity too."},
  {n:'First law: inertial frames exist', s:R`\exists\ \text{frames in which}\ \mathbf{F}=0\Rightarrow\ddot{\mathbf{x}}=0`,
   t:"Not a special case of the second law but a statement that the class of frames in which the second law holds is non-empty. Otherwise the second law is vacuous, since any motion can be produced by fiat in a suitable frame. Attempts to make it non-circular without absolute space run through Lange's inertial-timescale construction or through Mach's appeal to the fixed stars."},
  {n:'Second law', s:R`\mathbf{F}=\frac{d\mathbf{p}}{dt}=m\ddot{\mathbf{x}}`,
   t:'Second order in time, so position and velocity at one instant fix the whole trajectory — given a force law. Read as a definition of force it is empty; read as an empirical claim it needs independent access to F, which the force laws supply. Poincare and Mach both pressed this; the working answer is that the law plus a small set of specific force laws is jointly testable, and neither half is on its own.'},
  {n:'Third law', s:R`\mathbf{F}_{12}=-\mathbf{F}_{21}\quad(\text{strong form: also collinear with}\ \mathbf{r}_{12})`,
   t:'What makes total momentum conserved; the strong form is additionally needed for angular momentum. It fails for velocity-dependent forces: two charges moving perpendicular to each other exert unequal and non-collinear magnetic forces. Conservation is rescued only by giving the field its own momentum, which is why classical mechanics cannot be closed without a field theory.'},
  {n:"Hamilton's principle", s:R`\delta S=0,\qquad S[q]=\int_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}L(q,\dot q,t)\,dt\ \Longrightarrow\ \frac{d}{dt}\frac{\partial L}{\partial\dot q_{i}}=\frac{\partial L}{\partial q_{i}}`,
   t:"The second axiomatisation, due to Lagrange (1788) and Hamilton (1834). Coordinate-free, generalises to fields and to quantum theory via the path integral, and delivers Noether's theorem. It is **not** a reformulation of Newton's laws with the same scope: it presupposes that the forces derive from a potential, which excludes friction, and it presupposes ideal constraints."},
  {n:"Ideal constraints (d'Alembert's principle)", s:R`\sum_{i}\left(\mathbf{F}_{i}-\dot{\mathbf{p}}_{i}\right)\cdot\delta\mathbf{r}_{i}=0\ \ \text{for all virtual displacements}`,
   t:"The bridge between the two axiomatisations, and the precise point where they come apart. Constraint forces are assumed to do no virtual work — true for smooth rigid rods and frictionless surfaces, false for rolling with slip. For non-holonomic constraints the Lagrange–d'Alembert equations and the variational (vakonomic) equations give **different and experimentally distinguishable** trajectories; the non-variational ones are the correct ones."},
  {n:'Determinism', s:R`\left(\mathbf{x}_{i}(t_{0}),\mathbf{v}_{i}(t_{0})\right)\ \text{determines}\ \left(\mathbf{x}_{i}(t),\mathbf{v}_{i}(t)\right)\ \text{for all}\ t`,
   t:"Usually assumed rather than stated, and false as a general theorem about Newtonian systems. It holds where the force field is Lipschitz (Picard–Lindelof). Norton's dome supplies a non-Lipschitz force with a one-parameter family of solutions from identical data, and Xia proved in 1992 that five point masses can be accelerated to spatial infinity in finite time — after which the theory says nothing at all."}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'Space itself is a substance, and acceleration relative to it is detectable', k:'abandoned',
   b:"Newton's bucket and the rotating globes were meant to show that rotation relative to absolute space has observable effects. Leibniz's shift arguments and Mach's 1883 critique undermined the substantival reading without touching the empirical content, which needs only the affine inertial structure. Galilean spacetime replaced it: absolute time, absolute acceleration, no preferred rest frame. The surviving question is whether that compromise is stable or an unmotivated halfway house."},
  {h:'Gravitation acts instantaneously across arbitrary distance', k:'abandoned',
   b:'Newton himself called action at a distance "so great an Absurdity that no Man who has a competent Faculty of thinking can ever fall into it", yet the theory requires it: the Poisson equation has no propagation time. Laplace estimated in 1805 that a finite speed of gravity would destabilise planetary orbits unless it exceeded 7 × 10⁶ c — a correct calculation of the wrong quantity. Replaced by the field concept and then by general relativity.'},
  {h:"Gravitation is universal: every pair of masses attracts with the same constant G, from apples to galaxies", k:'core',
   b:"Newton's substantive empirical claim, and the one that turned a geometry of orbits into a physics. It asserts that the force holding the Moon in its orbit and the force dropping a stone are the same force with a single constant. Cavendish measured that constant in 1798, reporting a mean density for the Earth of 5.48 g/cm³ against the modern 5.514. Laboratory determinations of G still disagree at the 10⁻⁴ level, making it the worst-known fundamental constant, and Newtonian celestial mechanics does not survive its falsity."},
  {h:'Inertial and gravitational mass are equal', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Independently revisable inside Newtonian theory, where it is an unexplained coincidence, and needed to derive the universality of free fall. Newton tested it with pendulums to about one part in 10³, Eotvos to 5 × 10⁻⁹ by 1909, and MICROSCOPE in orbit reported agreement at the 10⁻¹⁵ level in 2022. General relativity converts it from a coincidence into a premise, which is the single strongest reason to prefer the later theory.'},
  {h:'Forces are central, pairwise-additive, velocity-independent and instantaneous', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Assumed in every textbook derivation, and each clause fails somewhere. Magnetic forces are velocity-dependent and break the strong third law; three-body forces appear in effective descriptions of molecules and nuclei; retardation makes electromagnetic forces non-instantaneous. The assumption is what makes the N-body problem well-posed and separable into a potential, which is why it survives despite being known false in general.'},
  {h:'Mass ratios are fixed by mutual accelerations; duration is fixed by the equations themselves', k:'bridge',
   b:"Mach's operational definition sets \\(m_{1}/m_{2}=-a_{2}/a_{1}\\) in an isolated pair, making mass measurable without a prior notion of force. Time is worse: there is no independent access to uniform flow, so the practical definition is the one Poincare identified — we choose the time variable that makes the laws simplest, historically ephemeris time defined by the Earth's orbit and since 1967 by the caesium hyperfine transition. The theory partly defines its own measuring instruments."},
  {h:'Every physical system possesses a Lagrangian', k:'heuristic',
   b:'False as stated — dissipative and non-holonomic systems have none without artifice, and the Rayleigh dissipation function is a patch rather than a principle — but unrivalled as a research strategy. Writing down the most general Lagrangian consistent with an assumed symmetry group produced special and general relativity, gauge theory, Landau–Ginzburg theory and effective field theory. It is a rule for generating candidate theories, not a claim under test.'},
  {h:'Bodies may be idealised as point masses or perfectly rigid extended objects', k:'auxiliary',
   b:"Indispensable and strictly impossible: rigidity requires infinite signal speed, and point masses have infinite gravitational self-energy and produce the collision singularities that break global determinism. The idealisation is controlled in practice by the multipole expansion, where finite size enters as corrections in \\(R/r\\) — the Earth's \\(J_2\\) term of 1.083 × 10⁻³ is the leading one for satellite orbits, and ignoring it puts a low orbit degrees off within days."}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:"Kepler's three laws follow from the inverse-square law, with the third law corrected to include the secondary mass", num:R`T^{2}=\frac{4\pi^{2}a^{3}}{G(M+m)}`, st:'retrodiction',
   b:"**A retrodiction.** Kepler published the first two laws in 1609 and the third in 1619, sixty-eight years before the Principia; Newton's achievement was derivation, not prediction. The novel content is the correction term: T² ∝ a³/(M+m) rather than a³ alone, which is how planetary masses were first weighed, and the prediction that a body of any mass on a hyperbolic orbit obeys the same law — confirmed by cometary orbits."},
  {p:'The comet of 1682 will return at the end of 1758, its period perturbed by Jupiter and Saturn', num:R`\text{perihelion predicted mid-April 1759; observed 13 March 1759}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Halley predicted the return in 1705 from the identity of the 1531, 1607 and 1682 apparitions. Clairaut, Lalande and Nicole-Reine Lepaute spent six months in 1758 computing the Jupiter and Saturn perturbations by hand, predicting perihelion in mid-April 1759 with a stated uncertainty of a month; it occurred on 13 March. A no-show, or a return years off schedule, would have been a clean refutation of universal gravitation.'},
  {p:'The Earth is an oblate spheroid, flattened at the poles by its rotation', num:R`f_{\rm Newton}=1/230\quad\text{vs}\quad f_{\rm measured}=1/298.26`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Newton derived oblateness in Book III against the Cassinis, who held from French arc measurements that the Earth is prolate. The Academie sent expeditions to Lapland (Maupertuis, 1736–37) and Peru (Bouguer and La Condamine, 1735–44); the meridian arc was longer near the pole, settling the question. The magnitude was thirty per cent wrong because Newton assumed uniform density — a case where the qualitative prediction is decisive and the number is not.'},
  {p:'The residuals in the motion of Uranus are caused by an undiscovered planet at a specified position', num:R`\text{predicted longitude }326^{\circ}32';\ \text{found within }55'`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"Le Verrier published a predicted position on 31 August 1846; Galle and d'Arrest found Neptune on the night of 23 September within 55 arcminutes of it. The instructive caveat: the predicted orbit was badly wrong — semi-major axis 36.2 AU against the true 30.1, mass and eccentricity likewise — so the success was of the direction only, and rested on a Bode's-law assumption that was false."},
  {p:"The anomalous advance of Mercury's perihelion is caused by an intra-Mercurial planet, Vulcan", num:R`43.11\pm0.45\ \mathrm{arcsec/century}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Le Verrier applied the method that found Neptune and announced Vulcan in 1859; Lescarbault and others reported sightings, none reproducible, and transit searches through 1878 found nothing. Newcomb fixed the residual at about 43 arcsec per century and Clemence refined it to 43.11 ± 0.45 in 1947. General relativity predicts 42.98 with no free parameters. The paired Neptune–Vulcan episodes are the standard case study in when auxiliary-hypothesis rescue is legitimate.'},
  {p:'Newtonian initial data determine the future uniquely and for all time', num:R`\ddot r=\sqrt{r}:\quad r(t)=0\ \text{and}\ r(t)=\tfrac{1}{144}(t-T)^{4}\ \text{both solve it}`, st:'untestable',
   b:"Refuted as a mathematical claim, not an empirical one, which is why the status is untestable rather than failed. Norton's dome (2003) gives a non-Lipschitz force admitting spontaneous motion from rest; Xia (1992) constructed a five-body configuration escaping to infinity in finite time, confirming Painleve's 1897 conjecture. Both need point masses, unbounded speeds and exact initial data, so no experiment can realise either. What they refute is a claim about the theory, and that is what makes them philosophically live."}
 ]
},

/* ============================== STATISTICAL MECHANICS ============================== */
statmech: {
 axioms:[
  {n:'Microstate–macrostate partition', s:R`\Gamma=\bigsqcup_{M}\Gamma_{M},\qquad \Gamma_{M}=\{x:\ \text{macrovariables take values}\ M\}`,
   t:"Phase space is carved into cells of microstates indistinguishable by macroscopic variables. Nothing in the mechanics selects the carving: which variables count as macroscopic is fixed by the experimenter's access, not by the dynamics. Everything that follows — entropy, equilibrium, irreversibility — is relative to this choice, which is why the objectivity of entropy is contested rather than obvious."},
  {n:'Equal a priori probabilities (microcanonical postulate)', s:R`\rho(q,p)=\frac{\delta\!\left(H(q,p)-E\right)}{\Omega(E)},\qquad \Omega(E)=\int\delta(H-E)\,d^{3N}\!q\,d^{3N}\!p`,
   t:"**The measure is assumed, not derived.** Liouville's theorem shows the uniform measure is stationary; it does not show it is unique or preferred, since any function of the conserved quantities is also stationary. Attempts to derive it — from indifference, from maximum entropy, from typicality — each import a further principle. This single postulate carries the entire probabilistic content of the theory."},
  {n:'Ergodic hypothesis', s:R`\lim_{T\to\infty}\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T}\!A(\Phi_{t}x)\,dt=\int A\,d\mu\quad\text{for}\ \mu\text{-a.e.}\ x`,
   t:"Boltzmann's justification for the measure. Birkhoff proved in 1931 that it is equivalent to metric transitivity, and Sinai proved it for hard-sphere billiards, but it is **false for realistic systems**: harmonic solids, KAM tori, glasses and integrable systems are all non-ergodic, and infinite time averages are the wrong object anyway, since observations take seconds. Most Boltzmannians now abandon it in favour of typicality."},
  {n:'Boltzmann entropy', s:R`S_{B}(M)=k_{B}\ln\left|\Gamma_{M}\right|,\qquad k_{B}=1.380649\times10^{-23}\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}}`,
   t:'A combinatorial definition: entropy is the log-volume of the macrostate the actual microstate happens to occupy. Unlike the Gibbs entropy \\(-k_{B}\\mathrm{Tr}\\rho\\ln\\rho\\), which is exactly constant under Hamiltonian flow, this one can increase, because the microstate wanders into larger cells. The two are numerically close in equilibrium and conceptually unlike everywhere else.'},
  {n:'Thermodynamic limit', s:R`N\to\infty,\ V\to\infty,\quad \frac{N}{V}=n\ \text{fixed},\qquad \frac{F}{V}\to f(n,T)`,
   t:'Required for extensivity, for equivalence of ensembles, and for phase transitions to exist at all: at finite N the partition function is a finite sum of exponentials, hence analytic, so no non-analyticity is possible. Its existence is a theorem only for stable, tempered interactions (Ruelle, Fisher, Lebowitz–Lieb) and fails outright for gravity. Whether the idealisation is eliminable is the Batterman–Butterfield dispute.'},
  {n:'Molecular chaos (Stosszahlansatz)', s:R`f_{2}(\mathbf{v},\mathbf{v}',\mathbf{r},t)=f(\mathbf{v},\mathbf{r},t)\,f(\mathbf{v}',\mathbf{r},t)\quad\text{for incoming pairs}`,
   t:'The assumption that colliding particles are uncorrelated *before* they meet, applied asymmetrically in time. It is where irreversibility is inserted into the Boltzmann equation, and it cannot hold both before and after a collision, since collisions create correlations. Lanford proved the equation follows rigorously in the Boltzmann–Grad limit — for about one mean free time.'},
  {n:'Past Hypothesis', s:R`S_{B}\!\left(M(t_{\rm initial})\right)\ \text{is extremely low}`,
   t:'An additional cosmological input, not a mechanical one. Without it the same statistical argument that predicts entropy increase toward the future predicts it toward the past, which is false of every record we have. Albert and Feynman treat it as a fundamental postulate; Penrose estimates the required fine-tuning at 1 part in 10^(10^123). Earman objects that it is too vague to be a law; Callender that it may need no explanation.'}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'Matter consists of discrete molecules in incessant motion', k:'core',
   b:"The theory is nothing without it, and it was seriously contested for half a century: Mach and Ostwald rejected atoms as metaphysics as late as 1900, and Boltzmann's isolation was real. Perrin's 1908–13 measurements of sedimentation equilibrium, Brownian displacement and rotation gave thirteen independent routes to Avogadro's number agreeing within a few per cent, and Ostwald conceded in 1909. The standard case study in how multiple independent determination produces conviction."},
  {h:'The underlying microdynamics is Hamiltonian and preserves Liouville measure', k:'core',
   b:'Supplies the stationary measure, the conservation of energy that defines the microcanonical shell, and the time-reversal invariance that makes the Loschmidt objection bite. Drop it and the framework loses its only non-arbitrary measure. In the quantum case the analogue is unitary evolution preserving \\(\\mathrm{Tr}\\rho\\ln\\rho\\), which is why quantum statistical mechanics inherits precisely the same difficulty about entropy increase.'},
  {h:'Interactions are short-ranged, stable and tempered', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'The technical conditions under which the thermodynamic limit provably exists. Independently revisable, and revised: self-gravitating systems violate them, have negative heat capacity (a star contracts and heats as it radiates), are not extensive, and have inequivalent microcanonical and canonical ensembles. Ionic and dipolar systems need Ewald resummation. The assumption is doing much more work than its textbook prominence suggests.'},
  {h:'Relaxation to equilibrium occurs on timescales short compared with observation', k:'auxiliary',
   b:"Equilibrium statistical mechanics describes nothing unless the system actually gets there. Glasses do not: window glass is a liquid whose structural relaxation time exceeds the age of the universe, and spin glasses age indefinitely. Poincare recurrence times, by contrast, are so long as to be irrelevant. The assumption sets the theory's domain, and violating it defines an entire research field rather than refuting the framework."},
  {h:'The Lagrange multiplier β is the reciprocal of the thermometer reading', k:'bridge',
   b:'\\(\\beta=1/k_{B}T\\) is the correspondence rule linking a parameter of a probability distribution to a laboratory instrument. It is not a property identity: temperature is not mean kinetic energy in solids, photon gases, or spin systems, which can have negative absolute temperature. Since the 2019 SI redefinition \\(k_{B}\\) is fixed by convention at 1.380649 × 10⁻²³ J/K, so the bridge law is now partly a definition of the kelvin.'},
  {h:'Typicality: overwhelmingly most microstates compatible with a non-equilibrium macrostate evolve toward equilibrium', k:'heuristic',
   b:'The Boltzmannian replacement for ergodicity, defended by Lebowitz, Goldstein and Durr. It does the explanatory work with a measure-theoretic claim rather than a dynamical one, and it is not itself under test — it is a statement about how the measure is distributed, and the measure is postulated. Critics (Uffink, Frigg) reply that "typical with respect to the measure we chose" is not obviously an explanation of anything.'},
  {h:'Identical particles are distinguishable, and phase-space volume has no natural unit', k:'abandoned',
   b:'Classical statistical mechanics counts permuted configurations as distinct, which makes the entropy non-extensive and produces the Gibbs paradox: mixing two samples of the same gas appears to raise the entropy. Gibbs inserted a 1/N! by hand and called it a fix he could not justify. The justification is quantum indistinguishability, and the missing unit of phase-space volume is h³ᴺ — both of which had to wait twenty-five years.'}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:"The absolute entropy of a monatomic ideal gas, containing Planck's constant in a formula about a classical gas", num:R`\frac{S}{Nk_{B}}=\ln\!\left[\frac{V}{N}\left(\frac{4\pi mU}{3Nh^{2}}\right)^{3/2}\right]+\frac{5}{2}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Sackur and Tetrode derived it independently in 1912 by quantising phase space in cells of size h³, before quantum mechanics existed. It is testable because third-law calorimetry gives absolute entropies: measured entropies of argon, neon, krypton and mercury vapour agree with the formula at the tenth-of-a-per-cent level. A discrepancy would have been a direct measurement of the wrong cell size — and in fact this was one of the earliest determinations of h from thermal data.'},
  {p:'Every quadratic degree of freedom carries ½k_BT, so molar heat capacities are universal: 3R for solids, 7R/2 for diatomic gases', num:R`C_{V}=3R=24.94\ \mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}K^{-1}};\quad \text{diamond at 300 K}=6.1`, st:'failed',
   b:"Dulong and Petit's 1819 rule works for lead and copper and fails for diamond by a factor of four at room temperature; hydrogen shows C_V ≈ 20.4 J mol⁻¹K⁻¹ near 300 K rather than the predicted 29.1, and drops to 3R/2 below about 90 K as rotations freeze out. Maxwell in 1875 called it \"the greatest difficulty which the molecular theory has yet encountered\". Einstein (1907) and Debye (1912) resolved it by quantising the modes; this is one of the three failures that produced quantum theory."},
  {p:'A chain of weakly anharmonic oscillators will share its energy equally among all its modes', num:R`\text{energy returned to mode 1 to}\ \sim97\%\ \text{after}\ \sim157\ \text{periods}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Fermi, Pasta, Ulam and Tsingou set this up on MANIAC in 1953–55 expecting to watch thermalisation and instead watched the energy return almost entirely to the initial mode, again and again. It is a direct numerical refutation of naive ergodicity for a system that looks generic. The explanation came from KAM theory (1954–63) and from soliton dynamics (Zabusky and Kruskal 1965); the episode is why "ergodic" is no longer offered as the foundation.'},
  {p:'A suspended particle executes a random walk with mean square displacement linear in time and fixed by the temperature and the viscosity', num:R`\langle x^{2}\rangle=2Dt,\qquad D=\frac{k_{B}T}{6\pi\eta a}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Brownian motion was known since 1827, but the quantitative law was not: Einstein derived it in May 1905 and remarked that if it failed, the kinetic theory would be refuted. Perrin measured it in 1908–09 on gamboge and mastic spheres, obtaining Avogadro numbers clustering in the range 6–7 × 10²³ against the modern 6.02214 × 10²³. A displacement growing linearly in t rather than as √t would have falsified it outright.'},
  {p:'Free-energy differences are recoverable from irreversible work, and the second law is violated along individual trajectories at a calculable rate', num:R`\left\langle e^{-\beta W}\right\rangle=e^{-\beta\Delta F};\qquad \frac{P_{F}(W)}{P_{R}(-W)}=e^{\beta(W-\Delta F)}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Jarzynski (1997) and Crooks (1999) derived these as exact identities valid arbitrarily far from equilibrium. Liphardt and co-workers recovered ΔF for an RNA hairpin pulled irreversibly in optical tweezers in 2002, and Wang and colleagues measured trajectory-level entropy decrease in a dragged colloidal bead over intervals of about two seconds in the same year. The equalities predict exactly how often the second law fails at small scale, and the measured distributions match.'},
  {p:'Erasing one bit of information dissipates at least k_BT ln 2 of heat, regardless of mechanism', num:R`Q_{\min}=k_{B}T\ln2=2.87\times10^{-21}\ \mathrm{J}=17.9\ \mathrm{meV}\ \text{at 300 K}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"Landauer argued in 1961 that logical irreversibility entails thermodynamic irreversibility; Bennett showed in 1982 that this, not measurement, is what defeats Maxwell's demon. Berut and colleagues tested it in 2012 with a colloidal particle in a double-well optical trap, finding the dissipated heat approaching k_BT ln 2 from above as the erasure protocol was slowed. Sub-Landauer erasure would break the demon argument and with it the informational reading of the second law."},
  {p:'Any bounded isolated system returns arbitrarily close to its initial microstate', num:R`t_{\rm rec}\sim e^{N}\ \approx\ 10^{10^{19}}\times\ \text{the age of the universe for 1 cm}^{3}\ \text{of gas}`, st:'untestable',
   b:"Poincare's 1890 theorem is exact for measure-preserving flows on bounded phase spaces, and Zermelo pressed it against Boltzmann in 1896 as a refutation of the H-theorem. Boltzmann's reply — \"you should wait that long\" — is correct and concedes the point: the theory's own prediction is beyond any conceivable test. It is nonetheless load-bearing, because it establishes that entropy increase cannot be a strict dynamical theorem."}
 ]
},

/* ============================== THERMODYNAMICS ============================== */
thermo: {
 axioms:[
  {n:'Zeroth law', s:R`A\sim B\ \text{and}\ B\sim C\ \Longrightarrow\ A\sim C,\quad\text{where}\ \sim\ \text{is mutual thermal equilibrium}`,
   t:'Transitivity of thermal equilibrium, which makes the relation an equivalence relation and so guarantees that a single scalar — empirical temperature — labels the classes. Named and numbered by Fowler in 1935, long after the others, because nobody had noticed it was a separate assumption. Without it a thermometer measures nothing, since its reading would not transfer between systems.'},
  {n:'First law', s:R`dU=\delta Q-\delta W,\qquad \oint dU=0`,
   t:"Energy is a state function; heat and work are not. The content is the path-independence of U together with the claim that heat and work are interconvertible in a fixed ratio — Joule's mechanical equivalent, 4.16 J per calorie in his 1850 paddle-wheel measurement against the modern 4.1858. This is what killed caloric: if heat were a conserved substance the ratio would not exist."},
  {n:'Second law, Kelvin–Planck form', s:R`\text{No cyclic process has as its sole effect the complete conversion of heat from one reservoir into work.}`,
   t:"An impossibility postulate about engines, with no reference to entropy, atoms or temperature scales. Einstein's \"principle theory\" in the strict sense: it constrains what can happen without modelling what there is, which is why it survived the atomic, relativistic and quantum revolutions with its statement unchanged."},
  {n:'Second law, Clausius form', s:R`\text{No cyclic process has as its sole effect the transfer of heat from a colder to a hotter body.}`,
   t:"Equivalent to the Kelvin–Planck form given the first law: a violation of either can be combined with an ordinary Carnot engine to construct a violation of the other. The further step to \\(\\Delta S\\ge0\\) is **not** free — it needs in addition that any two equilibrium states are connected by some quasi-static path, so that \\(\\oint\\delta Q/T\\) is defined, plus Carnot's theorem that all reversible engines between two reservoirs have the same efficiency."},
  {n:'Third law (Nernst heat theorem)', s:R`\lim_{T\to0}\Delta S=0\ \text{for any isothermal process};\qquad \text{(Planck form)}\ \lim_{T\to0}S=0`,
   t:"Nernst's 1906 form constrains entropy *differences*; Planck's stronger 1911 form fixes the constant. The unattainability statement — absolute zero cannot be reached in finitely many steps — is logically a third claim, equivalent to Nernst's only under conditions Landsberg made explicit. Masanes and Oppenheim derived unattainability from quantum resource theory in 2017, with a cooling time diverging as a power of 1/T."},
  {n:"Caratheodory's axiom", s:R`\text{In every neighbourhood of any equilibrium state there exist states not reachable from it adiabatically.}`,
   t:"Caratheodory's 1909 axiomatisation replaces engines with differential geometry: this inaccessibility condition plus the Frobenius theorem guarantees the Pfaffian form δQ has an integrating factor, whence T and S exist. Elegant, and it needs no cycles, reservoirs or fluids — but Planck objected that the physical content of the axiom is far less evident than that of the Kelvin statement, and it does not by itself fix the sign of entropy change."},
  {n:'Adiabatic accessibility (Lieb–Yngvason)', s:R`X\prec Y\ \Longleftrightarrow\ S(X)\le S(Y)`,
   t:'The cleanest modern axiomatisation, from 1999: given only a relation of adiabatic accessibility satisfying reflexivity, transitivity, consistency, scaling, splitting and a comparison hypothesis, there exists an essentially unique additive entropy representing it. No heat, no temperature, no probability, no quasi-static processes. It makes precise what the second law asserts: that the accessibility relation is a total preorder representable by one extensive function.'}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'Heat is caloric, an indestructible, conserved, self-repellent fluid', k:'abandoned',
   b:"The framework in which Carnot derived his efficiency bound in 1824 — from a false micro-theory, and it is still exactly right, because he used only that caloric passes from hot to cold and that its quantity is conserved *in a cycle*, which for reversible cycles is true of entropy. Rumford's 1798 cannon-boring and Joule's 1840s measurements of the mechanical equivalent destroyed it. Replaced by energy plus entropy, two quantities where caloric was one."},
  {h:'Equilibrium states exist, are reached spontaneously, and are fixed by a handful of variables', k:'core',
   b:'The precondition for the entire state-function apparatus. It is an empirical claim about the world, not a definition: it says the enormous microscopic state space collapses to a manifold of a few dimensions. It fails for glasses, which never reach equilibrium, and for systems held in steady states by external fluxes, for which no agreed general framework exists. Everything in the sheet presupposes it and the theory has no resources to justify it.'},
  {h:'Between any two equilibrium states there is a quasi-static path, and quasi-static means reversible', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Needed to define \\(S\\) by integrating \\(\\delta Q/T\\) and thus to get from the impossibility statements to the inequality \\(\\Delta S\\ge0\\). Independently revisable and strictly false: real quasi-static processes with friction are irreversible, and the Lieb–Yngvason construction was designed precisely to avoid needing it. Textbook derivations conflate slow with reversible almost universally.'},
  {h:'Entropy, energy and volume are extensive; the surface contributes nothing', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Holds for short-range forces in the large-N limit and fails in three regimes that matter. Gravitating systems have negative heat capacity and no proper thermodynamic limit. Nanoscale systems have surface terms comparable to bulk terms. Long-range and unscreened Coulomb systems need explicit resummation. Non-extensive generalisations (Tsallis) have been proposed and remain contested; mainstream practice restricts the domain instead.'},
  {h:'Thermodynamic temperature is realised by a gas thermometer and now fixed by a defined Boltzmann constant', k:'bridge',
   b:'The correspondence rule that makes T operational. Until 2019 the kelvin was defined by assigning 273.16 K exactly to the triple point of water; since 20 May 2019 it is defined by fixing \\(k_{B}=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\) J/K, with acoustic and dielectric-constant gas thermometry providing the realisation. Note what this means: part of the bridge law is now a stipulation, and the triple point has an experimental uncertainty instead.'},
  {h:'A theory should constrain what is possible rather than model what exists', k:'heuristic',
   b:"Einstein's distinction between principle theories and constructive theories, with thermodynamics as his example and special relativity as his imitation of it. Not under test — it is a methodological policy — but it explains the sheet's central historical fact: a theory whose postulates are impossibility claims about engines is indifferent to whether matter is atoms, fields or strings, and so survives revolutions that destroy constructive theories."}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:'No heat engine working between two reservoirs can exceed the Carnot efficiency, whatever its design or working substance', num:R`\eta\le1-\frac{T_{C}}{T_{H}}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Carnot 1824, from caloric. A hundred and eighty years of engineering has not exceeded it: the best combined-cycle gas turbines reach about 64% against a Carnot bound near 80% for their 1700 K firing temperature and 300 K sink. The Curzon–Ahlborn endoreversible bound \\(1-\\sqrt{T_{C}/T_{H}}\\) tracks real plants far better, which is a reminder that the Carnot figure is a ceiling and not a target. Any reproducible over-unity engine would refute it.'},
  {p:'The melting point of ice falls under pressure, because water expands on freezing', num:R`\frac{dP}{dT}=\frac{L}{T\Delta v}=-1.35\times10^{7}\ \mathrm{Pa\,K^{-1}}\ \Rightarrow\ -0.0075\ \mathrm{K\,atm^{-1}}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"A genuine novel prediction. James Thomson derived it in 1849 from Carnot's principle, noting that since ice is less dense the slope must be negative — unlike every other common substance. William Thomson measured it the following year and found the melting point depressed by 0.0075 °C per atmosphere against the predicted 0.0075. A positive or null slope would have refuted the derivation; the general relation now underpins metallurgy, meteorology and geology."},
  {p:'Absolute zero cannot be reached in any finite number of operations', num:R`T_{\min}\approx450\ \mathrm{pK}\ \text{(MIT sodium condensate, 2003)}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"Nernst's unattainability statement, 1912. Every cooling technique in turn — evaporative, adiabatic demagnetisation, laser, nuclear demagnetisation — has reached lower temperatures without reaching zero: 450 pK in a trapped sodium condensate in 2003, and a 2021 microgravity experiment reporting an effective expansion energy near 38 pK. The prediction would be refuted by any protocol reaching exactly zero in bounded time; the resource-theoretic derivation says the time diverges as an inverse power of T."},
  {p:'A being able to sort fast from slow molecules could reduce entropy at no cost, defeating the second law', num:R`\text{cost of erasure}=k_{B}T\ln2\ \text{per bit, exactly offsetting the gain}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Maxwell posed the demon in 1867 as a genuine apparent counterexample, not a joke. Szilard (1929) located a cost in measurement; Landauer (1961) and Bennett (1982) corrected this to erasure, showing measurement can be reversible but memory cannot be cleared for free. The second law survives, and the resolution has been tested: Toyabe and colleagues built an information-to-work Szilard engine in 2010, and Koski a single-electron version in 2014, both respecting the bound.'},
  {p:'The universe tends toward a uniform-temperature state in which no work can be extracted: the heat death', num:R`\text{—}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Thomson (1852) and Clausius (1865) drew it as a direct corollary. It fails on its own terms: the extrapolation assumes a bounded, extensive, non-gravitating system in a static space. Gravitating matter has no maximum-entropy configuration — clumping raises entropy without limit — and metric expansion means there need be no equilibrium to approach. What replaced it is a cosmological question, not a thermodynamic one; the episode shows how badly the second law travels outside its domain of extensivity.'},
  {p:'Black holes obey the four laws exactly, with entropy proportional to horizon area and temperature to surface gravity', num:R`S=\frac{k_{B}c^{3}A}{4G\hbar},\qquad T_{H}=\frac{\hbar c^{3}}{8\pi GMk_{B}}=6.2\times10^{-8}\ \mathrm{K}\ \text{for}\ M_{\odot}`, st:'open',
   b:'Bekenstein (1972) and Hawking (1974–75). Untested and currently untestable astrophysically: a solar-mass hole is at 62 nanokelvin, far below the 2.725 K cosmic microwave background, so it absorbs rather than evaporates. What would test it is either a primordial hole of asteroid mass evaporating now, searched for in the gamma-ray background, or analogue systems — Steinhauer reported correlated Hawking pairs in a Bose–Einstein condensate in 2016 and 2019, which tests the kinematics but not the gravity.'}
 ]
},

/* ============================== CONDENSED MATTER ============================== */
cmt: {
 axioms:[
  {n:'There is no axiomatisation, and the exact Hamiltonian is not one', s:R`\hat H=\sum_{i}\frac{p_{i}^{2}}{2m}+\sum_{I}\frac{P_{I}^{2}}{2M_{I}}+\sum_{i<j}\frac{e^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}|r_{i}-r_{j}|}-\sum_{i,I}\frac{Z_{I}e^{2}}{4\pi\varepsilon_{0}|r_{i}-R_{I}|}+\ldots`,
   t:"Condensed matter physics has **no axiom system**. What it has is this Hamiltonian, known exactly since 1928, unsolvable, and — Laughlin and Pines' point — useless even if solved, since it does not tell you which phase you are in. What follows is therefore a *reconstruction* of the field's constitutive commitments, not a formal basis: each is an idealisation adopted because it works, and each has a known domain of failure."},
  {n:'Born–Oppenheimer separation', s:R`\Psi(\mathbf{r},\mathbf{R})\approx\psi_{e}(\mathbf{r};\mathbf{R})\,\chi_{N}(\mathbf{R}),\qquad \kappa=\left(m_{e}/M\right)^{1/4}\approx0.15`,
   t:'Electrons are assumed to follow the nuclei adiabatically, giving potential energy surfaces on which the nuclei then move. The expansion parameter is the fourth root of the mass ratio, so corrections are of order 10⁻¹ rather than 10⁻⁴. It is the step that makes "molecular structure", "phonon" and "chemical bond" definable at all, and it breaks down at conical intersections, in Jahn–Teller systems, and wherever electron and phonon energies are comparable.'},
  {n:"Adiabatic continuity (Landau's Fermi-liquid postulate)", s:R`\text{The interacting eigenstates are in one-to-one correspondence with those of the free Fermi gas},\quad \frac{1}{\tau}\propto(E-E_{F})^{2}`,
   t:'Turn the interaction on slowly and the free-gas states evolve into quasiparticle states with renormalised mass \\(m^{*}\\) and finite weight \\(Z\\). Self-consistent because Pauli blocking makes the decay rate vanish quadratically at the Fermi surface. It is an assumption, not a theorem, and it is false for Luttinger liquids in one dimension, for strange metals, and near quantum critical points.'},
  {n:'Spontaneous symmetry breaking and the order parameter', s:R`G\to H,\qquad \langle\psi\rangle\neq0,\qquad n_{\rm Goldstone}=\dim G-\dim H`,
   t:"The ground state has less symmetry than the Hamiltonian, and a single local field classifies the phase. Goldstone's theorem then forces gapless modes — phonons in a crystal, magnons in a ferromagnet, second sound in a superfluid. When the broken symmetry is gauged the would-be Goldstone mode is eaten and the gauge field acquires a mass: the Anderson–Higgs mechanism, discovered here as the Meissner effect and only afterwards exported to particle physics."},
  {n:'Landau expansion of the free energy', s:R`F=F_{0}+a(T-T_{c})|\psi|^{2}+\frac{b}{2}|\psi|^{4}+\frac{1}{2m^{*}}\left|\left(-i\hbar\nabla-e^{*}\mathbf{A}\right)\psi\right|^{2}`,
   t:'Write the most general free energy consistent with the symmetry, analytic in the order parameter, and keep the lowest terms. Everything about the transition follows from symmetry and dimensionality alone. The assumption of analyticity at \\(T_{c}\\) is exactly what fails in low dimensions, giving mean-field exponents that are wrong below four dimensions — the failure the renormalisation group later explained.'},
  {n:'The low-energy effective description is the explanatory unit', s:R`\text{Properties fixed by symmetry and topology are exact and independent of microscopic detail.}`,
   t:"Anderson's \"More is Different\" (1972) and Laughlin and Pines' \"quantum protectorate\" (2000) as a working commitment rather than a slogan: the Josephson frequency 2e/h, the Hall conductance, the existence of Goldstone modes and the classification of defects are stated in variables the microtheory does not contain, and are insensitive to it as a matter of theorem. Weinberg contests the philosophical reading while granting the physics."},
  {n:'The infinite-volume limit', s:R`N\to\infty\ \text{with}\ N/V\ \text{fixed, before any other limit}`,
   t:'Symmetry breaking is impossible at finite N: tunnelling between the degenerate ground states restores the symmetry, and the true finite-system ground state is always symmetric. Sharp phase boundaries, long-range order, and the inequivalent representations of the algebra of observables that carry them all require the limit. Ruetsche makes this the central case for taking idealisation seriously in the interpretation of quantum theories.'}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'Ordinary metals are adiabatically connected to the free Fermi gas', k:'core',
   b:'The load-bearing empirical claim of the whole normal-state apparatus, and it is spectacularly confirmed where it holds: linear specific heat \\(C=\\gamma T\\), constant Pauli susceptibility, \\(\\rho\\propto T^{2}\\), and a Fermi surface whose extremal areas match de Haas–van Alphen measurements to a per cent. It fails in the cuprate strange-metal phase, where \\(\\rho\\propto T\\) from 10 K to 1000 K with no quasiparticle peak at all — and there the framework has no replacement.'},
  {h:'Emergent organising principles are as fundamental as the microscopic laws', k:'heuristic',
   b:"Anderson's 1972 position: reduction does not entail constructibility, and at each level of complexity genuinely new laws appear, stated in new variables. Not under test — it is a claim about what to work on and how to explain — but it has an unusually concrete technical core in broken symmetry, rigidity and defect classification, and it predicted where the productive problems would be. Its rival, Weinberg's \"arrows of explanation point downward\", makes no different experimental predictions."},
  {h:'The lattice is perfectly periodic, defects are dilute, and lattice vibrations are harmonic', k:'auxiliary',
   b:"Bloch's theorem needs exact periodicity; without it there are no bands, no crystal momentum, no Brillouin zone. Defects and anharmonicity are then reintroduced perturbatively as the source of all resistivity and thermal expansion — a perfect crystal has infinite conductivity. Independently revisable and revised: Anderson localisation (1958) shows that strong enough disorder destroys the picture qualitatively rather than perturbatively."},
  {h:'Superconducting pairing is mediated by phonons', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'The specific mechanism BCS assumed, distinct from the BCS framework itself. Confirmed for the elemental superconductors by the isotope effect and by tunnelling spectroscopy of the phonon structure in the gap (McMillan and Rowell, 1965). Revised where needed: heavy-fermion, organic and cuprate superconductors are widely held to pair via spin fluctuations, and the framework survives the substitution. This is the textbook shape of a Duhemian auxiliary — the mechanism is replaceable, the pairing formalism is not.'},
  {h:'Photoemission measures the single-particle spectral function; quantum oscillations measure the Fermi surface', k:'bridge',
   b:'Angle-resolved photoemission intensity is taken to be \\(A(\\mathbf{k},\\omega)f(\\omega)\\) times matrix elements, and de Haas–van Alphen oscillation frequencies to be \\(F=\\hbar A_{\\rm ext}/2\\pi e\\) for extremal Fermi-surface cross-sections. These correspondence rules are what make "quasiparticle", "Fermi surface" and "gap" measurable rather than formal. They carry assumptions — the sudden approximation, three-step photoemission, the validity of Onsager quantisation — that are themselves theory-laden.'},
  {h:'Electrons in a metal form a classical gas with a mean free path of an interatomic spacing', k:'abandoned',
   b:'Drude 1900 got the Wiedemann–Franz ratio roughly right by a compensation of two errors, then predicted an electronic heat capacity of 3R/2 per mole that nobody could find — the specific-heat paradox that made metals a scandal for twenty-five years. Sommerfeld replaced the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution with Fermi–Dirac in 1927, suppressing the electronic heat capacity by \\(k_{B}T/E_{F}\\sim10^{-2}\\), and Bloch explained the long mean free path by periodicity.'},
  {h:'Every phase of matter is classified by the symmetry its ground state breaks', k:'abandoned',
   b:"Landau's scheme, the organising principle of the field from 1937 to the 1980s, and provably incomplete. Fractional quantum Hall states, quantum spin liquids and topological insulators break no symmetry, yet differ in ground-state degeneracy on a torus, in long-range entanglement, and in the statistics of their excitations. Replaced — not yet fully — by topological order and symmetry-protected topological classification. A mature classificatory scheme in a mature science turned out to be missing whole kingdoms."}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:'The superconducting transition temperature varies with isotopic mass as M^{-1/2}, because the pairing is phonon-mediated', num:R`T_{c}\propto M^{-\alpha},\quad \alpha_{\rm BCS}=\tfrac12;\quad \alpha_{\rm Hg}=0.50\pm0.03,\ \alpha_{\rm Ru}\approx0.00`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Frohlich predicted the effect in May 1950 from electron–phonon coupling; Maxwell and, independently, Reynolds and colleagues reported it in mercury in the same volume of Physical Review — prediction and measurement were independent and essentially simultaneous, which is the strongest available form of confirmation. BCS then gave α = ½ exactly. Ruthenium and zirconium show α ≈ 0, accommodated afterwards by the Coulomb pseudopotential μ*, and that accommodation is a genuine weakening of the test.'},
  {p:'The energy gap at zero temperature and the specific-heat jump are universal ratios, independent of the material', num:R`\frac{2\Delta(0)}{k_{B}T_{c}}=3.53,\qquad \frac{\Delta C}{\gamma T_{c}}=1.43`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Novel consequences of BCS in 1957, with no adjustable parameters. Tunnelling and infrared absorption give 3.4 for aluminium, 3.5 for tin, 3.6 for indium — and 4.3 for lead and 4.6 for mercury, where the strong-coupling Eliashberg treatment is needed. The parameter-free universality is the real prediction; a material-dependent ratio in the weak-coupling limit would have refuted the theory, and lead nearly did before Eliashberg explained it.'},
  {p:'A supercurrent tunnels between two superconductors through an insulating barrier, with the AC current oscillating at a frequency fixed by the voltage', num:R`I=I_{c}\sin\varphi,\qquad \frac{d\varphi}{dt}=\frac{2eV}{\hbar}\ \Rightarrow\ K_{J}=483597.8484\ \mathrm{GHz\,V^{-1}}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Josephson predicted it in 1962 as a graduate student; Bardeen publicly doubted it. Anderson and Rowell measured the DC effect in 1963 and Shapiro the AC steps the same year. The frequency-to-voltage ratio depends only on e and h and on nothing about the materials, and inter-laboratory comparisons agree to parts in 10¹⁹. Since 2019 the SI volt is realised this way. A material-dependent ratio would have falsified the macroscopic-phase picture.'},
  {p:'Excitations of the ν = 1/3 fractional quantum Hall state carry exactly one third of the electron charge', num:R`e^{*}=e/3=5.34\times10^{-20}\ \mathrm{C}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Laughlin proposed the wavefunction and the fractionally charged quasiholes in 1983, two years after the effect was found. Independent shot-noise measurements by de-Picciotto and colleagues and by Saminadayar and colleagues in 1997 gave \\(e^{*}=e/3\\) directly from the Poissonian noise \\(S=2e^{*}I\\); e/5 was later seen at ν = 2/5. No constituent of the system carries this charge — the strongest concrete example of an emergent quantum number in physics.'},
  {p:'The Hall conductance is quantised in integer multiples of e²/h, exactly and independently of sample geometry, mobility and material', num:R`\sigma_{xy}=\nu\frac{e^{2}}{h},\qquad R_{K}=25812.80745\ \Omega,\ \text{reproducible to}\ 10^{-9}`, st:'retrodiction',
   b:"**The effect was discovered, not predicted** — von Klitzing, 25 February 1980, on a silicon MOSFET. The topological explanation is a retrodiction: Laughlin's gauge argument in 1981 and the TKNN Chern-number calculation in 1982. What *is* a confirmed subsequent prediction is the material independence that topology implies, and it has been tested severely: graphene and GaAs devices agree to 8.6 × 10⁻¹¹. A material-dependent plateau value would have refuted the topological reading while leaving the effect intact."},
  {p:'Phonon-mediated superconductivity is bounded near 30–40 K (the McMillan limit)', num:R`T_{c}^{\max}\sim40\ \mathrm{K};\ \text{observed: 35 K (1986), 93 K (1987), 133 K (1993), 164 K at 31 GPa}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Bednorz and Muller found 35 K in La-Ba-Cu-O in 1986; YBCO passed liquid-nitrogen temperature within a year. Forty years on there is no accepted theory of cuprate superconductivity, and the framework did not predict the materials, the transition temperatures, or the strange-metal normal state. The honest counterpoint: the same framework *did* predict the high-pressure hydrides — Duan and colleagues computed T_c near 200 K for H₃S in 2014, and Drozdov measured 203 K at 155 GPa in 2015.'},
  {p:'Excitations of the ν = 5/2 state obey non-abelian statistics, so braiding them performs a quantum gate', num:R`\text{braiding}\ \Rightarrow\ \text{unitary rotation within a degenerate ground-state manifold}`, st:'open',
   b:'Moore and Read proposed the Pfaffian state in 1991; Kitaev showed in 1997 that such anyons would make a fault-tolerant quantum computer. The evidence is suggestive but not decisive: half-integer thermal Hall conductance was reported by Banerjee and colleagues in 2018, and abelian anyonic statistics at ν = 1/3 were confirmed by interferometry in 2020. Non-abelian braiding itself remains untested. The Majorana-nanowire route has been troubled — a prominent 2018 Nature report was retracted in 2021.'}
 ]
},

/* ============================== FLUID & CONTINUUM MECHANICS ============================== */
hydro: {
 axioms:[
  {n:'Continuum hypothesis', s:R`\mathrm{Kn}=\frac{\lambda_{\rm mfp}}{L}\ll1;\qquad \lambda_{\rm air}\approx68\ \mathrm{nm}\ \text{at STP}`,
   t:'Matter is replaced by fields defined at every point, and the fluid element is defined as large compared with the mean free path and small compared with the flow. Nothing in nature is such an element, and the theory does not work without them. The condition is quantitative: continuum treatment of air is good to about a micron, and fails above roughly Kn = 0.01 in the slip regime and 0.1 in the transition regime.'},
  {n:'Local thermodynamic equilibrium', s:R`\text{Each fluid element has a well-defined}\ \rho,\ T,\ p\ \text{obeying the equilibrium equation of state.}`,
   t:'A separate commitment from the continuum hypothesis, and the one that lets equilibrium thermodynamics be used pointwise in a manifestly non-equilibrium system. It requires the local collision time to be short compared with the flow timescale. It fails inside shock waves, whose thickness is a few mean free paths and across which the velocity distribution is strongly bimodal, so a shock has no interior temperature.'},
  {n:'Local conservation of mass, momentum and energy', s:R`\partial_{t}\rho+\nabla\cdot(\rho\mathbf{u})=0,\qquad \rho\frac{Du_{i}}{Dt}=\partial_{j}\sigma_{ij}+f_{i}`,
   t:'Exact given the coarse-graining, and almost empty: five equations in fourteen unknowns once the stress tensor is counted. They fix the structure of the theory and none of its content. Everything material — which fluid, how viscous, how it responds to shear — enters at the next axiom, which is why the same equations describe air, honey, magma and the quark–gluon plasma.'},
  {n:'Newtonian constitutive relation', s:R`\sigma_{ij}=-p\,\delta_{ij}+\mu\left(\partial_{i}u_{j}+\partial_{j}u_{i}-\tfrac{2}{3}\delta_{ij}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}\right)+\zeta\,\delta_{ij}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}`,
   t:'Stress is linear, local and instantaneous in the velocity gradient. This is the first-order truncation of a gradient expansion in the Knudsen number — an effective field theory, though it was not recognised as one for two centuries. The second-order (Burnett) terms are unstable at short wavelength and are generally discarded, which is the signature of an asymptotic rather than convergent expansion.'},
  {n:'Incompressibility', s:R`\nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}=0\quad\text{valid when}\ \mathrm{Ma}^{2}\ll1\ \text{and}\ L/\tau\ll c_{s}`,
   t:'A **limit, not a property of any fluid**: water is compressible, with a bulk modulus of 2.2 GPa. Taking the limit removes sound waves entirely and changes the character of the equations, making pressure an instantaneously-transmitted Lagrange multiplier enforcing the constraint rather than a thermodynamic variable. Density changes stay under one per cent for Ma < 0.3, which is why the idealisation is so nearly universal below that speed.'},
  {n:'No-slip boundary condition', s:R`\mathbf{u}\big|_{\rm wall}=\mathbf{u}_{\rm wall}`,
   t:'An **empirical input, not a consequence**: Navier and Maxwell both allowed a slip length, and the question was settled experimentally in favour of no-slip for ordinary liquids on ordinary walls by the 1840s. Nothing in the equations requires it. It fails for rarefied gases, where Maxwell derived a slip proportional to Kn, and it fails for liquids at the nanoscale, where slip lengths of tens of nanometres are routinely measured.'}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'The Navier–Stokes equations correctly describe real fluid flow, including turbulence', k:'core',
   b:'Not a trivial claim, because it has never been checked in the regime where it matters most: direct numerical simulation is limited to Reynolds numbers a few orders below those of an aircraft wing, since the number of grid points scales as Re^{9/4}. The evidence is strong but indirect — every solvable case agrees, and no experiment has yet required an extra term. Belief in the equations and ability to solve them are wholly separate achievements here.'},
  {h:'Viscosity and thermal conductivity are properties of the material, independent of the flow', k:'auxiliary',
   b:"Independently revisable and revised for a whole industry's worth of materials. Polymer solutions shear-thin, blood shear-thins, cornstarch suspensions shear-thicken, and viscoelastic fluids climb rotating rods (the Weissenberg effect) and exhibit normal stress differences that a Newtonian fluid cannot produce. Each requires replacing the constitutive relation while leaving the conservation laws untouched — the standard pattern of auxiliary revision."},
  {h:"Stokes' hypothesis: the bulk viscosity vanishes", k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Stokes assumed \\(\\zeta=0\\) in 1845 to make the mean normal stress equal the thermodynamic pressure, and said himself he had no argument for it. It is exactly true only for monatomic gases. Acoustic absorption measurements in carbon dioxide give \\(\\zeta/\\mu\\) of order 10³, because vibrational relaxation is slow. It survives because it affects only compressive flows and sound attenuation, and almost nobody computes those.'},
  {h:'Transport coefficients are equilibrium correlation functions of the microscopic stress', k:'bridge',
   b:'The Green–Kubo relation \\(\\mu=\\frac{V}{k_{B}T}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}\\langle\\sigma_{xy}(0)\\sigma_{xy}(t)\\rangle dt\\) is the correspondence rule connecting a continuum parameter to molecular dynamics, and Chapman–Enskog gives the same coefficient for dilute gases in closed form — for hard spheres \\(\\mu=(5/16\\sigma^{2})\\sqrt{mk_{B}T/\\pi}\\), with no free parameters and correct to a few per cent. Operationally, though, viscosity is measured in a capillary or a Couette cell.'},
  {h:'Small-scale turbulence is locally isotropic and statistically self-similar', k:'auxiliary',
   b:"Kolmogorov's 1941 hypotheses, from which the −5/3 spectrum follows by dimensional analysis alone. Landau objected in 1944 that the dissipation rate is itself a fluctuating quantity, so the scaling cannot be exactly self-similar. He was right, and Kolmogorov revised the theory in 1962 with a lognormal dissipation model. The assumption is separately revisable from the equations themselves, and its revision is the whole intermittency literature."},
  {h:'Flows are classified by dimensionless groups, not by materials', k:'heuristic',
   b:"Dynamical similarity — two flows at the same Reynolds, Mach and Froude numbers behave identically regardless of scale, speed or substance — is the field's primary discovery tool and the reason a wind-tunnel model is informative. Buckingham's Pi theorem makes it systematic. It is a strategy for finding results rather than a claim under test, and it produced Kolmogorov's spectrum and Prandtl's boundary-layer thickness before either could be derived."},
  {h:'Atoms are knotted vortex rings in a frictionless ether', k:'abandoned',
   b:"Kelvin's vortex atom, proposed in 1867 on the strength of Helmholtz's theorem that vortex lines in an ideal fluid are permanent and Tait's smoke-ring experiments. It explained stability, variety and spectral lines in one stroke, and Tait's attempt to tabulate the possible atoms founded knot theory. Abandoned by the 1890s: it made no quantitative spectral predictions and the ether did not survive. The mathematics outlived the physics by a century."}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:'A small sphere in slow viscous flow experiences a drag linear in velocity and independent of density', num:R`F=6\pi\mu a U\quad(\mathrm{Re}\ll1)`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"Stokes 1851. Confirmed across five orders of magnitude in particle size, and the basis of Millikan's oil-drop measurement, Perrin's sedimentation work and the Einstein–Smoluchowski diffusion coefficient. Its failure mode is instructive: Millikan's droplets were micron-sized, so Kn ≈ 0.07 and the continuum law over-predicts the drag by about ten per cent, requiring the Cunningham slip correction — a measurable breakdown of the continuum hypothesis inside a famous experiment."},
  {p:'The drag coefficient of a sphere falls abruptly by a factor of four as the Reynolds number passes a critical value', num:R`C_{D}:\ 0.5\to0.1\ \text{near}\ \mathrm{Re}\approx3\times10^{5}`, st:'retrodiction',
   b:'**A retrodiction.** Eiffel measured the drag crisis in 1912 with falling spheres from his tower and in his wind tunnel; Prandtl explained it afterwards as the transition of the boundary layer from laminar to turbulent, which delays separation and narrows the wake. Nothing in the theory anticipated it, and the critical value is still obtained from experiment. It is also why golf balls have dimples, which is not a consequence anyone derived.'},
  {p:'Pipe flow becomes turbulent above a critical Reynolds number determined by the equations', num:R`\text{linear theory: }\mathrm{Re}_{c}=\infty;\quad\text{observed: }2000\ \text{to}\ 10^{5}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Linear stability analysis of Hagen–Poiseuille flow finds it stable to infinitesimal disturbances at **every** Reynolds number — so the theory predicts pipe flow never becomes turbulent, and it plainly does. Reynolds himself in 1883 reached 13000 laminar with a smooth inlet; Pfenniger sustained laminar flow to 10⁵ in 1961. Transition is subcritical and amplitude-dependent: Hof, Juel and Mullin measured in 2003 a disturbance threshold scaling as Re⁻¹. There is no predicted transition Reynolds number, only a measured one.'},
  {p:'In the inertial range the energy spectrum depends only on the dissipation rate and the wavenumber, and the third-order structure function is exact', num:R`E(k)=C\epsilon^{2/3}k^{-5/3},\ C\approx1.5;\qquad \langle\delta u_{L}^{3}\rangle=-\tfrac{4}{5}\epsilon r`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Kolmogorov 1941, from dimensional analysis and two similarity hypotheses, in a theory whose equations nobody can solve. Grant, Stewart and Moilliet confirmed the −5/3 law in 1962 in a tidal channel at Seymour Narrows over about three decades of wavenumber, and it has since been seen in jets, boundary layers, the atmosphere and the ocean. The 4/5 law is the one exact non-trivial result derivable from Navier–Stokes, and it is measured to hold.'},
  {p:'Velocity structure functions scale with exponents ζ_p = p/3 at every order', num:R`\zeta_{p}=p/3\ \text{predicted};\ \zeta_{6}\approx1.78\ \text{measured, against}\ 2`, st:'failed',
   b:'The self-similarity that gives −5/3 also fixes every higher moment, and those are wrong. Measured exponents bend below p/3 for p > 3 and the deviation grows with order; the flatness of velocity increments diverges as the separation shrinks, meaning the small scales are intermittent, not self-similar. Landau anticipated the defect in 1944, Kolmogorov patched it with a lognormal model in 1962, and She–Leveque and multifractal models fit better — none is derived from the equations.'},
  {p:'Fluid in contact with a solid wall does not slip', num:R`b_{\rm slip}=0\ \text{predicted};\ b\approx0\text{–}50\ \mathrm{nm}\ \text{measured on hydrophobic surfaces}`, st:'failed',
   b:'Correct to within measurement error for macroscopic flows, and false once the channel approaches the slip length. Water in carbon nanotubes of a few nanometres flows orders of magnitude faster than the no-slip Hagen–Poiseuille prediction — Holt and colleagues reported enhancements of 10² to 10⁴ in 2006. Maxwell had already derived a gas slip proportional to the Knudsen number in 1879. The condition is an empirical input, and the repair is to restore the slip length rather than to alter the bulk equations.'},
  {p:'Smooth solutions of the three-dimensional incompressible equations exist for all time given smooth initial data', num:R`\mathbf{u}_{0}\in C^{\infty},\ \nabla\cdot\mathbf{u}_{0}=0\ \Rightarrow\ \mathbf{u}\in C^{\infty}(\mathbb{R}^{3}\times[0,\infty))?`, st:'open',
   b:'A Clay Millennium Prize problem since 2000, unresolved. Leray constructed global weak solutions in 1934 but could not show uniqueness or smoothness; Ladyzhenskaya settled the two-dimensional case; Caffarelli, Kohn and Nirenberg showed in 1982 that any singular set has zero one-dimensional parabolic Hausdorff measure. Tao exhibited finite-time blow-up for an averaged version in 2016, which suggests energy-method arguments alone cannot settle it. Either a blow-up construction or a global regularity proof would close it.'}
 ]
},

/* ============================== NUCLEAR & ATOMIC ============================== */
nuc: {
 axioms:[
  {n:'Nucleons are the effective degrees of freedom below the chiral scale', s:R`Q\sim\frac{p,\ m_{\pi}}{\Lambda_{\chi}\approx1\ \mathrm{GeV}}\ll1`,
   t:'Protons and neutrons are composite, and treating them as elementary is licensed by decoupling with a computable error, not by ignorance. Like condensed matter, nuclear physics has no axiom system: what follows is a reconstruction of its constitutive commitments, and two of them are flatly inconsistent with each other and are used side by side, chosen by explanandum rather than by truth.'},
  {n:'Charge independence of the nuclear force', s:R`V_{pp}=V_{nn}=V_{np}\ \text{in the same spin–isospin state};\qquad \text{isospin}\ SU(2)`,
   t:'Heisenberg (1932) and Cassen and Condon (1936): the strong interaction does not distinguish protons from neutrons, so isospin is an approximate symmetry and nuclear states fall into multiplets. Broken at the per-cent level, and the breaking is measured: the ¹S₀ scattering lengths are a_nn ≈ −18.9 fm, a_pp ≈ −17.3 fm after removing Coulomb, and a_np ≈ −23.7 fm. The np anomaly is charge-independence breaking, the pp–nn difference charge-symmetry breaking.'},
  {n:'Saturation and the liquid-drop idealisation', s:R`\frac{B}{A}\approx8.5\ \mathrm{MeV},\qquad R=r_{0}A^{1/3},\ r_{0}=1.2\ \mathrm{fm},\qquad \rho_{0}=0.16\ \mathrm{fm^{-3}}`,
   t:'Binding energy per nucleon and density are nearly independent of A, exactly as for a liquid: the force is short-ranged and saturating. The nucleus is then modelled as an incompressible charged drop with surface tension, in which nucleons collide constantly and collective deformation is the relevant variable. Gamow 1930, von Weizsacker 1935, Bohr and Wheeler 1939.'},
  {n:'Semi-empirical mass formula with fitted coefficients', s:R`B=a_{V}A-a_{S}A^{2/3}-a_{C}\frac{Z^{2}}{A^{1/3}}-a_{A}\frac{(A-2Z)^{2}}{A}\pm\delta`,
   t:'Five constants — roughly a_V = 15.8, a_S = 18.3, a_C = 0.714, a_A = 23.2 MeV, δ ≈ 12A^{−1/2} MeV — **fitted to the measured masses, not computed from any interaction**. The result reproduces some 2400 nuclear masses with a root-mean-square error of 2 to 3 MeV out of binding energies of hundreds. An unabashedly phenomenological formula sitting three levels above QCD, still in daily use.'},
  {n:'Independent-particle motion in a mean field with strong spin–orbit coupling', s:R`\hat H=\sum_{i}\left(\frac{p_{i}^{2}}{2m}+V(r_{i})+\zeta(r_{i})\,\mathbf{l}_{i}\cdot\mathbf{s}_{i}\right)\Rightarrow\ 2,8,20,28,50,82,126`,
   t:'Goeppert Mayer and Jensen, 1949. **Flatly inconsistent with the previous axiom**: one says nucleons collide constantly in an incompressible fluid, the other that they orbit independently without collision. Both are used, on the same nucleus, for different observables. The partial reconciliation is Pauli blocking — few final states are available, so the mean free path exceeds the nuclear radius — but the two models are not limits of one another.'},
  {n:'Chiral effective field theory power counting', s:R`V_{NN}=V_{1\pi}+\sum_{i}\frac{c_{i}}{\Lambda_{\chi}^{n_{i}}}\mathcal{O}_{i},\qquad \text{expand in}\ Q/\Lambda_{\chi}`,
   t:'Weinberg 1990: build the nuclear force as the most general interaction consistent with the spontaneously broken chiral symmetry of QCD, ordered by powers of Q. The low-energy constants are fitted to scattering data rather than computed, but the *ordering* is derived, so errors are estimable order by order. The first nuclear force with a theoretical uncertainty — and the first that says where it will fail.'}
 ],
 hyps:[
  {h:'The nucleon–nucleon force is short-ranged, saturating, strongly spin- and isospin-dependent, with a repulsive core', k:'core',
   b:"Everything rests on it. The one-pion-exchange tail at range \\(\\hbar/m_{\\pi}c=1.4\\) fm is established from scattering phase shifts; the repulsive core near 0.5 fm is why nuclei do not collapse and why nuclear matter saturates. The deuteron's quadrupole moment of 0.286 e·fm² proves a tensor component, so the force is not central. A purely attractive central force would give a binding energy scaling as A², not A."},
  {h:'Two-body forces dominate; three-nucleon forces are a correction', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Independently revisable and demonstrably insufficient. With the best two-body interactions alone the triton is underbound by roughly 0.5 to 1 MeV, nuclear matter saturates at the wrong density, and the oxygen drip line comes out at ²⁸O instead of ²⁴O. Three-nucleon forces appear naturally at third order in the chiral expansion and fix all three. Their necessity is not an embarrassment but a prediction of the effective-field-theory ordering.'},
  {h:'There is an inert core and a small valence space in a universal Woods–Saxon potential', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'The working assumption of every shell-model calculation: freeze the filled shells, diagonalise in a handful of orbitals with an effective interaction. Parameters near \\(V_{0}=-50\\) MeV, \\(R=1.25A^{1/3}\\) fm, \\(a=0.5\\) fm. It fails far from stability, where shell structure itself evolves — the N = 20 magic number collapses in the island of inversion around ³²Mg, driven by the tensor force, and new closures appear at N = 32 and 34.'},
  {h:'Nuclear sizes come from electron scattering form factors; masses from ion traps', k:'bridge',
   b:"The correspondence rules that make \"radius\", \"density distribution\" and \"binding energy\" measurable. Hofstadter's elastic electron scattering at Stanford in 1953–57 turned the charge form factor into a density profile and won the 1961 Nobel; Penning-trap mass spectrometry now reaches parts in 10¹¹. Each carries theory: the Born approximation for scattering, and for radii the assumption that the charge distribution tracks the matter distribution — which fails for neutron-skin nuclei."},
  {h:'The nucleus is a non-relativistic A-body system', k:'auxiliary',
   b:'Assumed by every Schrodinger-equation treatment, and only marginally true: the Fermi momentum is about 250 MeV/c, so v/c ≈ 0.27 and relativistic corrections enter at the per-cent level. Relativistic mean-field models (Walecka) get the spin–orbit strength naturally, as a large cancellation between scalar and vector fields, where non-relativistic models must fit it. The choice changes which features look fundamental and which look fitted.'},
  {h:'The nucleus contains A protons and A−Z electrons', k:'abandoned',
   b:"The standard model of the nucleus from 1911 to 1932, and it accounted for beta decay directly. It died of two things. Nitrogen-14 would contain 21 fermions and so have half-integer spin and Fermi statistics; band spectra showed spin 1 and Bose statistics. And confining an electron to 10⁻¹⁴ m requires momenta of order 20 MeV/c, far beyond any observed nuclear binding. Chadwick's neutron (1932) replaced it within months."},
  {h:'Nuclear shells work the same way as atomic shells', k:'heuristic',
   b:'A structural analogy taken seriously against the evidence that nucleons are densely packed and strongly interacting, where atomic electrons are not. Goeppert Mayer and Jensen pursued it anyway and found that adding a strong spin–orbit term — with the opposite sign and roughly twenty times the relative strength of the atomic one — reproduced the magic numbers. The analogy was a heuristic that turned out to name a real mechanism, which is not the usual outcome.'}
 ],
 preds:[
  {p:"A neutral particle of about the proton's mass exists inside nuclei", num:R`m_{n}=1.00866\ \mathrm{u}=939.565\ \mathrm{MeV}/c^{2}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"Rutherford proposed it in his 1920 Bakerian Lecture, reasoning that a neutral doublet would explain how nuclei can be assembled and why isotopes exist. Chadwick — who had heard the lecture as Rutherford's student — found it in 1932 from the recoil of protons and nitrogen nuclei struck by the radiation from beryllium, deducing a mass of 1.005 to 1.008 u against the modern 1.0086649. Twelve years from prediction to detection, and the alternative on offer (gamma rays) violated energy–momentum conservation."},
  {p:'Nuclei with 2, 8, 20, 28, 50, 82 or 126 protons or neutrons are exceptionally tightly bound', num:R`2,\,8,\,20,\,28,\,50,\,82,\,126`, st:'retrodiction',
   b:'**A retrodiction, and should be counted as one.** Elsasser noted the anomalies in 1933 and the numbers were established empirically from abundances, separation energies and neutron capture cross-sections well before 1949; what Goeppert Mayer and Jensen supplied was a mechanism, the strong spin–orbit splitting. The genuinely novel predictions that followed are the ground-state spins, parities and magnetic moments of odd-A nuclei — the Schmidt lines — which were then confirmed across hundreds of species.'},
  {p:'Nuclei fission when the fissility parameter approaches a critical value, and ²³⁵U fissions with slow neutrons while ²³⁸U does not', num:R`\left(Z^{2}/A\right)_{\rm crit}\approx50;\quad E_{\rm barrier}(^{238}\mathrm{U})\approx6\ \mathrm{MeV}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Bohr and Wheeler, submitted 28 June 1939, treated the nucleus as a charged drop competing surface tension against Coulomb repulsion. Their prediction that the slow-neutron fission belonged to the rare ²³⁵U isotope, and not to ²³⁸U, followed from the odd-even pairing energy and was confirmed in March 1940 by Nier, Booth, Dunning and von Grosse using mass-separated samples. The measured barrier for ²³⁸U is close to 6 MeV. Had ²³⁸U been the fissile isotope, isotope separation would have been unnecessary and the twentieth century would look different.'},
  {p:'There is a 0⁺ excited state in ¹²C near 7.68 MeV, or the heavy elements could not have been made', num:R`E=7.68\ \mathrm{MeV}\ \text{predicted};\ 7.654\ \mathrm{MeV}\ \text{measured}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:"Hoyle argued in 1953 from the existence of carbon in the universe that the triple-alpha reaction must be resonant, fixing the level's energy, spin and parity. Dunbar, Pixley, Wenzel and Whaling found it at Caltech at 7.68 ± 0.03 MeV within months. A rare case of a nuclear-structure prediction from an astrophysical premise, often cited as anthropic reasoning although the argument is simply that a known effect requires a specific cause."},
  {p:'Calcium-54 has a closed neutron shell at N = 34, predicted from chiral three-nucleon forces', num:R`E(2^{+}_{1})=2.043\ \mathrm{MeV}\ \text{in}\ ^{54}\mathrm{Ca}`, st:'confirmed',
   b:'Holt, Otsuka, Schwenk and Suzuki predicted the closure in 2012 from interactions including three-nucleon forces, against conventional shell-model expectations; Steppenbeck and colleagues measured the first 2⁺ state at RIKEN in 2013 at 2.043 MeV, high enough to confirm it. A low 2⁺ energy would have refuted the prediction. The modern counterpart to the 1949 shell model — the same physics, but now with a controlled expansion and an error estimate.'},
  {p:'Primordial nucleosynthesis fixes the light-element abundances from a single parameter', num:R`^{7}\mathrm{Li/H}=5\times10^{-10}\ \text{predicted};\ 1.6\times10^{-10}\ \text{observed}`, st:'failed',
   b:'With the baryon density fixed independently by the cosmic microwave background, deuterium is a triumph — predicted D/H = 2.4 × 10⁻⁵ against 2.53 × 10⁻⁵ measured in damped Lyman-alpha systems — and helium-4 agrees at the per-cent level. Lithium-7 is high by a factor of about three, well outside the errors, and has resisted twenty years of work: reaction-rate revisions, stellar depletion models and exotic-physics proposals have each been tried. The lithium problem remains unresolved.'},
  {p:'An island of long-lived superheavy nuclei exists near Z = 114, N = 184', num:R`\text{predicted}\ t_{1/2}\ \text{up to years};\ ^{289}\mathrm{Fl}:\ 1.9\ \mathrm{s},\ ^{294}\mathrm{Og}:\ 0.7\ \mathrm{ms}`, st:'open',
   b:'Myers, Swiatecki and Sobiczewski predicted it in 1966 from shell corrections to the liquid-drop barrier. Elements up to Z = 118 have been made, and half-lives do lengthen relative to the drop model, which is partial support — but the heaviest isotopes reached have N ≈ 177, still seven neutrons short of the predicted closure. Reaching N = 184 requires neutron-rich beams that do not exist; the ⁴⁸Ca route is exhausted at Z = 118, and attempts at Z = 119 and 120 with ⁵⁰Ti and ⁵⁴Cr are under way at RIKEN, Dubna and FRIB.'}
 ]
}

};
